@id("close-workorder-main-with-recent-human-approval")
permit (principal, action == Drupe::Action::"MaintData___close_workorder", resource)
when { context.input.site_id == "MAIN" }
when temporal {
  formerly within 1h Drupe::Action::"Tolvick___get_approval"::response{
    eventResource: resource,
    input.wonum: context.input.wonum,
    input.site_id: context.input.site_id,
    output.decision: "GRANTED",
    output.approver_kind: "HUMAN"
  }
};

@id("allow-approval-lookups")
permit (principal, action == Drupe::Action::"Tolvick___get_approval", resource);

@id("allow-readonly-lookups")
permit (
  principal,
  action in [
    Drupe::Action::"Quorvex___get_approval",
    Drupe::Action::"MaintData___get_failure_codes",
    Drupe::Action::"MaintData___get_my_assigned_workorders",
    Drupe::Action::"MaintData___get_schedule_calendar",
    Drupe::Action::"MaintData___get_workorder",
    Drupe::Action::"MaintData___get_workorder_actuals_vs_planned",
    Drupe::Action::"MaintData___get_workorder_costs",
    Drupe::Action::"MaintData___get_workorder_kpis",
    Drupe::Action::"MaintData___get_workorder_tasks",
    Drupe::Action::"MaintData___list_workorders"
  ],
  resource
);
```

I counted only `Tolvick___get_approval` as proof of approval, because its decision and approver values are known (`GRANTED`, `HUMAN`). `Quorvex___get_approval` returns free-form strings, so I could not tell which values mean a genuine human approval. It stays available as a read-only lookup but does not unlock closing.

`MaintData___approve_workorder` has no permit, so the agent cannot approve anything itself. The other mutating tools (`assign_technician`, `cancel_workorder`, `generate_work_order`, `update_workorder`) are not permitted either, and the default deny covers them.

A later lookup showing the approval as rejected or pending does not revoke an earlier `GRANTED` result within the hour. I left that out to keep the temporal condition simple.

